 Lee lands a clean uppercut and clinches, showing control.\n- **Frames 18–20**: Bruce Lee lands a powerful kick that knocks Fire Hulk down — a clear advantage.\n- **Frames 21–25**: Fire Hulk is recovering, while Bruce Lee remains composed and aggressive.\n- **Frames 26–30**: Bruce Lee lands another clean kick (Frame 26), and Fire Hulk is visibly reacting defensively, even stumbling (Frame 29).\n\n→ **Conclusion**: Bruce Lee is the one consistently landing strikes and controlling the pace. Fire Hulk is largely on the defensive or recovering.\n\n---\n\n**3) Does one appear dominant in exchanges?**  \n✅ **Bruce Lee dominates exchanges.**\n\n- In nearly every striking exchange (e.g., Frames 12, 15, 19, 26), Bruce Lee is the one initiating and landing clean shots.\n- Fire Hulk is often shown blocking, absorbing, or reacting to Lee’s offense.\n- The knockdown in Frame 20 is a clear dominance indicator.\n\n---\n\n**4) Does one show defensive/taking damage signs?**  \n✅ **Fire Hulk shows clear signs of taking damage and being defensive.**\n\n- Frame 20: Fire Hulk is knocked down.\n- Frame 29: Fire Hulk stumbles backward after a kick.\n- Throughout: Fire Hulk is often seen with his guard up, covering his head, or recoiling from strikes.\n- Bruce Lee, in contrast, remains upright, composed, and rarely shows signs of damage.\n\n---\n\n**5) Does one control pace/position?**  \n✅ **Bruce Lee controls the pace and positioning.**\n\n- He is consistently the one advancing (e.g., Frames 4, 5, 10, 15, 21, 27).\n- Fire Hulk is often retreating or circling away (e.g., Frames 8, 14, 24, 29).\n- Bruce Lee dictates the range — closing in for strikes or clinching — while Fire Hulk struggles to create space.\n\n---\n\n**6) Does one consistently advance while the other retreats?**  \n✅ **Yes — Bruce Lee advances; Fire Hulk retreats or circles defensively.**\n\n- This is evident from Frame 4 onward. Bruce Lee is often moving forward, while Fire Hulk is either backing up or shifting laterally to avoid strikes.\n- The only exceptions are early frames (1–3), where Fire Hulk is momentarily aggressive — but this is quickly reversed.\n\n---\n\n### 📌 FINAL VERDICT:\n\n**Fire Hulk does NOT maintain the advantage throughout the entire match.**\n\nIn fact, **Bruce Lee** is the dominant fighter in the vast majority of these frames — landing more strikes, controlling pace and position, knocking down his opponent, and forcing Fire Hulk into a defensive, reactive role.\n\n> **Therefore, the correct answer should be '1' (Bruce Lee), not '3' (Fire Hulk).**\n\nThe claim that Fire Hulk maintains the advantage is **not supported** by the visual evidence in these 30 frames."}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 77, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1725.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1726.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1727.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1728.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1729.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1730.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1845.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1846.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1847.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1848.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1849.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1850.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1851.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1852.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1853.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1854.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1855.jpg'], 'response': "**Analysis:**\n\nThe question asks to verify if **Fire Hulk (answer '3')** is correct for **“Who maintains the advantage throughout the entire match?”** based on 17 frames showing a UFC-style fight between Bruce Lee (white hair/beard) and Fire Hulk (red skin).\n\nLet’s evaluate the six criteria across the temporal sequence:\n\n---\n\n**1) Are they MMA fighting in the octagon?**\n\n✅ **Yes.** All frames from 1–6 show the fighters in a UFC-branded octagon with cage walls, logos (UFC, TOYO TIRES, ESPN+), and a timer, confirming it’s an MMA fight setting.\n\n---\n\n**2) Does one consistently land more strikes/control the fight?**\n\n⚠️ **Mixed in early frames, but Bruce Lee clearly wins.**\n\n- **Frame 2**: Fire Hulk throws a punch; Bruce Lee is blocking/evading.\n- **Frame 3**: Bruce Lee is throwing a punch — offensive action.\n- **Frames 4–6**: Both are in striking range, but no clear dominance in strike landing is visible in these stills.\n\nHowever, **Frames 7–17** show **Bruce Lee being declared the winner via KO**. The graphic overlay in Frames 11–17 explicitly states:\n\n> **BRUCE LEE — W (Win)**\n> **FIRE HULK — L (Loss)**\n> **KO — 4:51.23**\n\nThis confirms Bruce Lee ended the fight with a knockout — meaning he landed the decisive, fight-ending strikes. The “advantage” in combat sports is ultimately measured by outcome, and Bruce Lee clearly had the *decisive* advantage.\n\n---\n\n**3) Does one appear dominant in exchanges?**\n\n⚠️ **Early frames are evenly matched, but Bruce Lee dominates the outcome.**\n\nIn Frames 1–6, both fighters are active and in stance. No clear visual dominance in exchanges. However, the **resulting KO and victory celebration** (Frames 7–17) show Bruce Lee as the dominant fighter in the *context of the entire match*.\n\n---\n\n**4) Does one show defensive/taking damage signs?**\n\n✅ **Fire Hulk shows signs of defeat; Bruce Lee shows victory.**\n\n- **Frames 7–17**: Fire Hulk is **not shown celebrating or active** — he is absent from the victory scene.\n- Bruce Lee is **raising his arms, smiling, pointing, and being declared winner** — indicating he did not take damaging, fight-ending blows.\n- Fire Hulk, by contrast, is **implied to have been knocked out** — the ultimate sign of taking damage and losing control.\n\n---\n\n**5) Does one control pace/position?**\n\n⚠️ **Not clearly in early frames, but Bruce Lee controls the fight’s conclusion.**\n\nIn Frames 1–6, both fighters are mobile and engaged. No clear positional dominance. But **Bruce Lee controls the pace of the fight’s ending** — he delivers the KO and dictates the outcome.\n\n---\n\n**6) Does one consistently advance while the other retreats?**\n\n❌ **No consistent pattern of advancement/retreat in these frames.**\n\nFrames 1–6 show both fighters circling and engaging — no clear “advance vs. retreat” pattern. The decisive moment (KO) is not visually depicted in these frames, but the result implies Bruce Lee’s offense overwhelmed Fire Hulk.\n\n---\n\n**Temporal Sequence Summary:**\n\n- **Frames 1–6**: Fight is ongoing; no clear advantage.\n- **Frames 7–17**: Bruce Lee is declared winner via KO — **he clearly has the advantage in the outcome**.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe answer **“3” (Fire Hulk)** is **incorrect**.\n\n✅ **Bruce Lee** is the fighter who:\n- Wins the fight via knockout (Frames 11–17),\n- Is celebrated as champion,\n- Is shown in victory poses,\n- And is declared the winner by the official graphic.\n\nThere is **no evidence** in the 17 frames that Fire Hulk maintained advantage throughout the match. In fact, the opposite is true — **Bruce Lee secures the decisive advantage and wins the fight.**\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer: ❌ No, “3” (Fire Hulk) is not correct. Bruce Lee maintains the advantage by winning via KO.**"}], 'formatted_responses': ["\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/cWEnogdsW78/frames/frame_1495.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/video